</ul>

<p>Why do some people have bad taste?</p>
<ul>
  <li>They think taste is mostly subjective and that each person’s taste is as valid as the other.</li>
  <li>They confuse the appreciation of beauty with vanity, and there are afraid to develop it lest it renders then vain.</li>
  <li>They have taste in specific domains, but don’t bother improving their general taste.</li>
</ul>

<p>How do you develop taste?</p>
<ul>
  <li>Taste is not describable knowledge. It’s a skill, and like all skills, it is acquired at first through imitation, and refined with practice. It can be useful to think about taste like a sport.</li>
  <li>Learn to identify people with good taste.</li>
  <li>Slavishly copy what they do. Practice imitation. Don’t worry about understanding everything.</li>
  <li>Once you’ve got imitation down. Take time to understand.</li>
  <li>Notice beauty in all things and learn to seek it. Taste is a transferable trait.</li>
</ul>

